# Pyxis Gecko Source Pin

Decided: 2026-07-04
Revised: 2026-07-05 (pivoted off ESR — see "Why the pivot away from ESR 140" below)
Decided by: automated build-prep session (see gecko/BUILD_RUNBOOK.md for full context)

## Pin (current — this is what actually builds)

- Upstream product: **mozilla-central** (trunk/nightly), not ESR.
- Source repo: `https://github.com/mozilla/gecko-dev` (git mirror of
  `hg.mozilla.org/mozilla-unified`), same repo as originally chosen — see
  "Why gecko-dev over hg" below, unchanged.
- Branch: `master` (gecko-dev's mirror of mozilla-central/mozilla-unified's
  default branch), tracked locally as `origin/master`.
- Local working branch: **`central-work`**, created off `origin/master` to
  get off detached HEAD after the checkout (`git branch central-work HEAD`).
  Build and all further patch work happens on this branch.
- Pinned commit: `5836a062726f715fda621338a17b51aff30d0a8c`
  ("Bug 1960567 - remove the last C++ and scriptable APIs to accumulate data
  to legacy telemetry histograms, r=chutten") — this is the commit that
  **actually produced a working build** (`./mach build` completed cleanly,
  `obj-pyxis-webapp/dist/bin/vela --version` reports
  `Mozilla Firefox 142.0a1`). Re-resolve and re-pin explicitly on any
  deliberate rebase; do not silently float on `master`.
- Local checkout root: `target/gecko-src/gecko-dev/` (gitignored — see
  `.gitignore`'s `/target/` rule; this tree is multiple GB and must never be
  committed).
- Mozconfig: `gecko/mozconfig-vela` (in this repo, not inside the
  gitignored checkout) — see that file's inline comments for two flags that
  had to be removed to get a clean `./mach build` on this tree:
  `--disable-maintenance-service` (Windows-only, not a registered configure
  option on Linux) and `--enable-webrender` (fully removed from the tree —
  WebRender is unconditional/default-on now, there's nothing left to opt
  into).

## Why the pivot away from ESR 140

The original plan (below, kept for the record) picked ESR 140 on the
`release` branch for slow-moving stability. In practice, real
`./mach bootstrap` / `./mach build` attempts on that branch failed with:

    mozbuild.configure.options.InvalidOptionError:
    Could not find artifacts for a toolchain build named `toolchain-linux64-clang`

Traced via `python/mozbuild/mozbuild/artifact_commands.py`: Taskcluster only
indexes toolchain-build artifacts (prebuilt clang, clang-tidy, etc.) for
**"trunk projects"** (mozilla-central), not for release/ESR branches — this
is stated in the source's own comments, not a guess or a misconfiguration on
our end. Checking out an older commit on the same branch reproduced the
identical error, ruling out "bad/DONTBUILD commit" as the cause and
confirming it's structural to how Mozilla's CI indexes artifacts per-branch.

Fix: pivoted the whole checkout to mozilla-central via

    git remote set-branches origin '*'
    git fetch origin master
    git checkout origin/master -b central-work

This is a real, accepted trade-off away from the original ESR-stability
rationale: Pyxis is now tracking a nightly-churn trunk branch, which means
more frequent rebase pressure on the three Pyxis patches (protocol handler,
bridge actor, kiosk mode) than an ESR base would have required. Revisit if
Mozilla ever exposes toolchain artifacts for release/ESR branches, or if
rebase churn on `master` becomes a real cost — but do not revert silently;
any move back to ESR needs the same toolchain-artifact check re-verified
first, since that's what killed the ESR path this time.

## Original ESR 140 rationale (superseded, kept for context)

Per `pyxis-gecko-embedding-plan.md` §2: Pyxis wanted a slow-moving,
security-patched base with a predictable rebase cadence, not a 4-week nightly
churn — ESR was the natural choice for that reason, and 140 over 128 because
it had more runway (128.x was trailing off at 128.14.0 while 140.x was still
being actively released). This reasoning is still sound in the abstract; it
was simply blocked by the toolchain-artifact indexing gap above, which is a
CI/infrastructure constraint rather than a flaw in the reasoning itself.

Originally pinned commit on `release` (never successfully built — bootstrap
failed before a build was attempted against it):
`c61a6579702f4c73ee99131bd5fa216ec8f7b72c`.

## Why gecko-dev (git) over mozilla-unified (hg)

- This environment has no working `hg`/Mercurial install, and no interactive
  sudo to install one (`mach bootstrap` itself needs interactive sudo, so
  installing hg by hand only to then still be blocked on mach bootstrap
  doesn't buy anything in this pass).
- `git` and network access to github.com both work in this environment
  (verified: `git ls-remote` and `curl` against github.com and ftp.mozilla.org
  both succeeded).
- `gecko-dev` is Mozilla's own official read-only git mirror of
  mozilla-unified (github.com/mozilla/gecko-dev), kept in sync continuously —
  it is not a stale third-party fork. It is an accepted, commonly-used source
  for git-based Gecko contributors (`./mach` and the build system work
  identically regardless of whether the working copy came from hg or git —
  the build system does not require a live hg/VCS connection to build, only
  to run `mach mercurial-setup`-style workflows which Pyxis doesn't need).
- Tradeoff accepted: gecko-dev lacks the fine-grained per-build tags that
  hg.mozilla.org's releases repo has, so the pin here is a commit SHA on a
  branch, not a symbolic release tag. This is recorded explicitly above so a
  future rebase knows exactly what "the pin" means.

## Environment notes that mattered for a real build (not just a shallow read)

- **Python**: mach hardcodes `MAX_PYTHON_VERSION_TO_CONSIDER = (3, 12)` and
  fails on 3.14 with `AttributeError: 'Constant' object has no attribute 's'`
  during bootstrap. Fixed with `pyenv install 3.12.12 && pyenv local 3.12.12`
  in the gecko-dev checkout.
- **clang-tidy toolchain artifact**: even on mozilla-central, `mach bootstrap`
  failed trying to fetch `toolchain-linux64-clang-tidy`. Confirmed via
  `mozboot/bootstrap.py` that clang-tidy is only a static-analysis/dev-tool
  install, not required to compile the browser — proceeded straight to
  `./mach build` rather than chasing bootstrap to a clean exit, and the build
  succeeded anyway.
- **Full, unshallowed clone was required** in practice, matching the original
  plan's expectation below.

## Clone strategy used this session

Full clone of mozilla-unified/gecko-dev is very large (multi-GB, long git
history spanning the Netscape/Mozilla/B2G lineage). This session did a
**shallow, single-branch clone** to make source available for inspection
(validating the `nsIProtocolHandler.idl` shape, `moz.build` conventions, etc.)
without spending the whole session budget on transfer, then later widened the
remote's branch set and fetched `master` directly (see pivot above) rather
than re-cloning from scratch.

Expect a real build-ready checkout to take a long time and a large amount of
disk (tens of GB for full history with all objects); disk budget was checked
at pin time and had 482GB free, which is ample headroom.
